{"authored" [{"question" "q-74233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the multiplicity of the root $x=1$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-5x+3?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722" "basis" 314 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-74233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x=1$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n${x}^{3}$\n${x}^{2}$\n${x}^{1}$\n${x}^{0}$ \n\n \n$1$\n$1$\n$1$\n$-5$\n$3$\n\n \n\n$↓$\n$1$\n$2$\n$-3$\n\n \n\n$1$\n$2$\n$-3$\n$0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-1}={x}^{2}+2x-3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}+2x-3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-1)(x+3)(x-1) \\\\  & =(x+3)(x-1{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-1)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=1$ has multiplicity $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722" "basis" 314 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-74233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/activity-metadata.jso