emy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-178256" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to factor the polynomial. For this polynomial, we can factor by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & ={x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}-25x+125 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-5)-25(x-5) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-25)(x-5) \\\\  & =(x+5)(x-5)(x-5) \\\\  & =(x-5{)}^{2}(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(x-5)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x+5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-5$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722" "basis" 314 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-178256" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4801635bfbb5ee30a4382305fa41763e5af67e3d2ccc33354922d9e5ae6f64e2" "basis" 314 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-178256" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722" "basis" 314 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-178256" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-5"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722" "basis" 314 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-178256" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-5,\\,5"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722" "basis" 314 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-178256" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=-\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722" "basis" 314 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-178256" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=5"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722" "basis" 314 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-178256" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\frac{1}{5}"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722" "basis" 314 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-271146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The multiplicity of the root $x=4$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+14x-8$ is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722" "basis" 314 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-271146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x=4$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-4)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n${x}^{3}$\n${x}^{2}$\n${x}^{1}$\n${x}^{0}$ \n\n \n$4$\n$1$\n$-7$\n$14$\n$-8$\n\n \n\n$↓$\n$4$\n$-12$\n$8$\n\n \n\n$1$\n$-3$\n$2$\n$0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-4}={x}^{2}-3x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}-3x+2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-4)(x-1)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-4)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=4$ has multiplicity $1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722" "basis" 314 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-271146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4801635bfbb5ee30a4382305fa41763e5af67e3d2ccc33354922d9e5ae6f64e2" "basis" 314 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-271146" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722" "basis" 314 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-271146" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722" "basis" 314 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-74233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the multiplicity of the root $x=1$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}+{x}^{2}-5x+3?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-74233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x=1$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-1)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-1)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-1)$ using synthetic division:\n\n${x}^{3}$\n${x}^{2}$\n${x}^{1}$\n${x}^{0}$ \n\n \n$1$\n$1$\n$1$\n$-5$\n$3$\n\n \n\n$↓$\n$1$\n$2$\n$-3$\n\n \n\n$1$\n$2$\n$-3$\n$0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-1}={x}^{2}+2x-3,\n$$\n\nwhich means that\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-1)({x}^{2}+2x-3).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-1)(x+3)(x-1) \\\\  & =(x+3)(x-1{)}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-1)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=1$ has multiplicity $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-74233" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4801635bfbb5ee30a4382305fa41763e5af67e3d2ccc33354922d9e5ae6f64e2"} {"question" "q-74233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-74233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-74233" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-112815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=7{x}^{3}+42{x}^{2}$ have multiplicity $2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-112815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to factor the polynomial. \n\nFor this polynomial, we can factor by noting that the greatest common factor of $7{x}^{3}$ and $42{x}^{2}$ is $7{x}^{2}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =7{x}^{3}+42{x}^{2} \\\\  & =7{x}^{2}(x+6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $x$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x+6)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-6$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=0$ has multiplicity $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-112815" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4801635bfbb5ee30a4382305fa41763e5af67e3d2ccc33354922d9e5ae6f64e2"} {"question" "q-112815" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-112815" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-6"] ["math" "x=-6,0"] ["math" "x=0"] ["math" "x=0,6"] ["math" "x=7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-112815" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-178254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)=27{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}-3x-1$ have multiplicity $1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-178254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to factor the polynomial. For this polynomial, we can factor by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =27{x}^{3}+9{x}^{2}-3x-1 \\\\  & =9{x}^{2}(3x+1)-(3x+1) \\\\  & =(9{x}^{2}-1)(3x+1) \\\\  & =(3x-1)(3x+1)(3x+1) \\\\  & =(3x-1)(3x+1{)}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(3x+1)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-\\frac{1}{3}$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(3x-1)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=\\frac{1}{3}$ has multiplicity $1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-178254" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4801635bfbb5ee30a4382305fa41763e5af67e3d2ccc33354922d9e5ae6f64e2"} {"question" "q-178254" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-178254" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-3"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{1}{3},\\,3"] ["math" "x=3"] ["math" "x=\\frac{1}{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-178254" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\frac{1}{3}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-178256" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which root(s) of the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}-25x+125$ have multiplicity $2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-178256" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, we need to factor the polynomial. For this polynomial, we can factor by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & ={x}^{3}-5{x}^{2}-25x+125 \\\\  & ={x}^{2}(x-5)-25(x-5) \\\\  & =({x}^{2}-25)(x-5) \\\\  & =(x+5)(x-5)(x-5) \\\\  & =(x-5{)}^{2}(x+5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the roots and their multiplicities, we look at the factors:\n\n- The factor $(x-5)$ has an exponent of $2$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $2.$\n\n- The factor $(x+5)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=-5$ has multiplicity $1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only the root $x=5$ has multiplicity $2.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-178256" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4801635bfbb5ee30a4382305fa41763e5af67e3d2ccc33354922d9e5ae6f64e2"} {"question" "q-178256" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-178256" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-5"] ["math" "x=-5,\\,5"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{1}{5}"] ["math" "x=5"] ["math" "x=\\frac{1}{5}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-178256" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=5"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-271146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The multiplicity of the root $x=4$ in the polynomial $p(x)={x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}+14x-8$ is{{field-1}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-271146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $x=4$ is a root of $p(x),$ the factor theorem tells us that $(x-4)$ is a factor of $p(x).$\n\nBecause $(x-4)$ is a factor of $p(x),$ we know that $(x-4)$ divides $p(x)$ with no remainder. So, to begin factoring $p(x),$ we can divide it by $(x-4)$ using synthetic division:\n\n${x}^{3}$\n${x}^{2}$\n${x}^{1}$\n${x}^{0}$ \n\n \n$4$\n$1$\n$-7$\n$14$\n$-8$\n\n \n\n$↓$\n$4$\n$-12$\n$8$\n\n \n\n$1$\n$-3$\n$2$\n$0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{p(x)}{x-4}={x}^{2}-3x+2,\n$$\n\nwhich means\n\n$$\np(x)=(x-4)({x}^{2}-3x+2).\n$$\n\nWe can factor the quadratic factor even further, and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}p(x) & =(x-4)(x-1)(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe factor $(x-4)$ has an exponent of $1$ in the factored form of $p(x).$ So the root $x=4$ has multiplicity $1.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13929491/state.json" "file_sha256" "8476230ac85df9c2708290a113e9fb2314cecbd071c678d2ff07c05abb3cf722"} {"question" "q-271146" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category